    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/tesla-semi-bursts-into-flames-fire-shuts-down-california-highway-277703.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/tesla-semi-bursts-into-flames-fire-shuts-down-california-highway-277703.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-10-28T18:26:03+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-28T18:26:03+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-28T19:17:49.157698+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-28T19:17:49.157698+00:00",
    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Returns to the Annual SEMA Show Featuring a 300-HP Sentra Spec-V Revival Concept, Two Z Cars\nTesla Semi Bursts into Flames, Fire Shuts Down California Highway\n8 photos\nPhoto: KTVU FOX 2 San Francisco | YouTube\nA Tesla Semi truck caught fire on an interstate in California, prompting a massive highway shutdown due to hazardous chemical smoke. It is the first known incident of its kind involving a Tesla Semi that combusted without being involved in a traffic collision.\nThe truck driver safely evacuated the vehicle before it was engulfed in flames. No injuries were reported, but the Semi was badly burned. Firefighters faced a fierce blaze that supposedly started in the vehicle’s high-capacity lithium-ion battery pack.\nThere is no information about the variant involved in the fire, but Tesla produces the Semi in two distinct flavors. The Long Range comes with the gigantic 822-kWh battery pack for a drive of up to 500 miles (805 kilometers) before it needs to be plugged in again, while the Standard Range is equipped with the 548-kWh battery pack, storing enough energy for a range of up to 325 miles (523 kilometers).\nTo put out the fire, the emergency response personnel deployed chemical foam, covering the tractor unit, while managing the risk of toxic airborne emissions. Firefighters successfully put out the fire faster than expected.\nPhoto: KTVU FOX 2 San Francisco | YouTube California Highway Patrol originally feared an 18-hour burn, similar to what they needed in the blaze that occured at the Frank Kent Cadillac dealership in Fort Worth, Texas, which took place back on October 9 and destroyed no fewer than six electric vehicles, all parked in the dealer's yard.\nAccording to the official Tesla Semi Emergency Response Guide, first responders are strictly instructed never to submerge the vehicle to extinguish it. Instead, they must monitor the high-voltage battery temperature using thermal imaging cameras and store the vehicle at least 50 feet away from any structures for 24 hours after the fire is put out.\nThe fire broke out around 5:35 P.M. on Tuesday in the southbound lanes of the I-880 near Route 92. The California Highway Patrol (CHP) completely closed both directions of the freeway by 5:45 P.M. Northbound lanes reopened at 8:40 P.M., while the southbound lanes remained blocked for nearly ten hours, fully reopening around 3:30 A.M. on Wednesday.\nThe cause remains under investigation. The driver told the California Highway Patrol officers that the big rig \" simply caught fire ,\" and he had no idea what triggered it, which points to a potential internal electrical or thermal fault in the high-voltage battery pack. The spontaneous combustion draws fresh scrutiny from the federal regulators.\nPhoto: CHP Hayward | X The incident follows a previous Tesla Semi fire in August 2024 near Emigrant Gap on Interstate 80. However, back then, the Semi caught fire after it went off the road and hit a tree, requiring 50,000 gallons of water and aerial fire retardant to cool down.\nThe incident is bad timing for Tesla. The firm has recently sent the Semi into production at the factory in Sparks, Nevada, just outside Reno, after multiple delays. Tesla received a major order last month. ZET SCALE Alliance, backed by Microsoft and PepsiCo, ordered 2,500 trucks on September 22, 2026, marking the largest single Class 8 electric truck order in history for North America\nTRAFFIC ALERT – I-880 FULL CLOSURE A Tesla semi-truck has caught fire on I-880, resulting in a complete shutdown of both northbound and southbound lanes due to the potential risk of hazardous chemical smoke. https://t.co/O01z32nxpZ — CHP Hayward (@CHPHayward) October 28, 2026\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTesla Semi Tesla tesla semi fire tesla semi production EV fire\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT NASCAR 26 Review (PS5): Make Another Left Turn!",
